# debug-servy — tunable settings for the cloud routine.
#
# This is the ONE place to change these values. The skill sources this file at
# the start of every run; do not hardcode any of them in SKILL.md again.
#
# Shell syntax: KEY=value, no spaces around '=', '#' starts a comment.

# Backlog throttle. The run skips the REVIEW (no clone, no scan, no posting) only when BOTH hold
# (see MAX_OPEN_SECURITY_BUG_ISSUES below for the second count and the OR escape hatch it adds):
#
#     open issues NOT claimed by the fix routine  >  MAX_OPEN_MAINTAINER_ISSUES
#     AND open issues labelled security or bug    >= MAX_OPEN_SECURITY_BUG_ISSUES
#
# "NOT claimed by the fix routine" = not assigned to FIX_ASSIGNEE and not labelled 'tests' - i.e.
# issues that only aelassas himself can close, as opposed to the routine's own fixable backlog
# (servy-fix lands these on main/net48 and closes them itself; they were never really "his" queue).
#
# WHERE 80 CAME FROM (measured 2026-09-05, see KNOWLEDGE.md "The backlog throttle" section for the
# full numbers). Over the aelassas/servy issue history, aelassas's TRUE maintainer-only closing rate
# (excluding anything assigned to the fix account or labelled 'tests') was ~1.3-1.4 issues/hour
# across 21 days, active every single day with no dry spells - his single busiest CALENDAR DAY ever
# recorded was 77 closures. 80 sits just above that all-time daily peak: the gate only engages once
# his standing maintainer-only queue is larger than even his best day could clear, so it protects
# against actually drowning him without cutting off supply while he still has healthy headroom.
#
# The BACKFILL SWEEP still runs even when throttled (it only labels/assigns existing issues and is
# deliberately placed before the throttle decision - see SKILL.md Step 1a-bis), as does the
# unanswered-user pass. A throttled run records the reason in the ledger: a normal, successful
# outcome, not an error.
#
# WHAT THE THROTTLE NO LONGER STOPS (operator, 2026-09-06 evening): findings that never reach the
# maintainer's plate. A `tests` finding is posted `fixable` (assigned to FIX_ASSIGNEE) and a
# comment/XML-doc finding leaves as a handoff patch - servy-fix lands both itself. Throttling those
# starved the fix routine for the wrong reason (the same mistake the 2026-09-05 redesign fixed one
# layer up). So a throttled scan run now drops into FIXABLE-ONLY MODE (SKILL.md Step 3-quater):
# only Q57 (seeded by real Codecov line numbers) and the comment lens, on at most
# FIXABLE_ONLY_MAX_FILES candidate files chosen by scripts/fixable-candidates.sh, reading only the
# uncovered ranges - not the 57x4 review. Nothing maintainer-bound is posted while throttled.
MAX_OPEN_MAINTAINER_ISSUES=80

# OR escape hatch on the throttle above (added 2026-09-08, operator instruction: "kan je hier een
# OF aan toevoegen. Als minder dan 15 security of bugs open staan, dan mag je de issues voor de
# maintainer ook posten" - can you add an OR here; if fewer than 15 security or bug issues are
# open, you may post the maintainer-bound issues too).
#
# The throttle above STOPS the review only when BOTH hold:
#     open issues NOT claimed by the fix routine  >  MAX_OPEN_MAINTAINER_ISSUES
#   AND
#     open issues labelled security or bug        >= MAX_OPEN_SECURITY_BUG_ISSUES
#
# i.e. a thin security/bug count overrides a full general queue: even with the maintainer's plate
# over threshold, the review still runs and posts to him when fewer than this many of his open
# issues are security or bug findings. The rationale is that those two categories are the ones
# least safe to let run dry, whatever the state of the general (mostly enhancement/quality) queue.
# The two labels are summed from two separate search calls, not one OR query - GitHub's search API
# does not reliably OR two label: qualifiers in a single query (measured 2026-09-08: a parenthesised
# "(label:a OR label:b)" query returned 0 even when each label alone was non-zero).
#
# 15 is the operator's own number, not a measured one like the 80 above - there is no equivalent
# closing-rate study behind it yet. Revisit if it turns out to fire too often or not often enough.
#
# ⚠️ MEASURED 2026-09-08: open security=0, open bug=0. The label mapping (KNOWLEDGE.md "Known label
# registry") gives a review finding EXACTLY ONE category label, and rules 2/4/5 of that mapping route
# almost everything to `tests` or `enhancement` - a true `bug`/`security` finding is comparatively
# rare (a similar 0-most-of-the-time pattern already burned the retired total-open+BUGSEC design, see
# .claude/skills/servy-scan/reference/history.md line 10 for that incident). Practically, this means
# the escape hatch will likely be BELOW 15 on most or even every throttled run, so it will fire far
# more often than not - the AND-throttle above may end up binding only when a real bug/security
# backlog has built up, not merely when the general queue is long. That is what was asked for
# ("as long as security/bug stays thin, keep posting even with a full general queue"), not a bug in
# the arithmetic - but it does mean this largely supersedes MAX_OPEN_MAINTAINER_ISSUES=80 as the
# practical gate most hours, rather than sitting behind it as a rare exception. Revisit 15 if that is
# not the intended balance once real data comes in.
MAX_OPEN_SECURITY_BUG_ISSUES=15

# Cap on candidate files a THROTTLED scan run reads in fixable-only mode. The cheap gate
# (fixable-candidates.sh: Codecov misses, minus files fully reviewed or already checked this cycle,
# minus files already named in an open `tests` issue) costs no reading; each surviving file then
# costs roughly 3-5k tokens (only the MISS ranges, the constructor and the sibling test file's
# mocks are read), against ~24k for a full-review file.
#
# WHY 8 - AN ESTIMATE, NOT A MEASUREMENT (operator asked, 2026-09-06). 8 x 3-5k = 24-40k plus
# overhead puts a throttled hour at ~30-50k: an order of magnitude under the 300k ceiling, about
# 2-3x the cost of the old skip. Nobody has measured a real fixable-only run yet. Step 3-quater
# logs `context_size` before and after the mode into the ledger note precisely so this number can
# be set from data after a few throttled runs; until then treat 8 as a budget guess. It also runs
# LAST in a throttled run - after the maintainer-first block below - so the context gate, not this
# cap, is what actually bounds it when the maintainer needed the budget.
# 0 disables the mode and restores the pre-2026-09-06 "throttled = skip" behaviour.
FIXABLE_ONLY_MAX_FILES=8

# Q57 DEDICATED SLOT, EVERY RUN - not just when throttled (added 2026-09-18, operator instruction:
# "geef Q57 een eigen slot" - give Q57 its own slot). Until this existed, general test-coverage
# supply (Q57, grounded in real Codecov misses across ALL of src/**, no project filter - the
# operator was explicit this is NOT scoped to specific projects) only got attention when the
# per-file rotation in Step 4 happened to land on question 57 for a file already in that run's
# worklist. Now SKILL.md Step 3-quater runs the SAME fixable-candidates.sh mechanism as the
# throttled-only fixable-only mode, but unconditionally, right after Step 3-ter and before Step 4,
# on every run - a small guaranteed slice of every hour spent specifically on coverage, on top of
# whatever Q57 finds on its own via the normal rotation.
#
# Cap for the NON-throttled, ADDITIVE case (the throttled case still uses FIXABLE_ONLY_MAX_FILES
# above, unchanged - there this mode remains the run's WHOLE review, not additive). Deliberately
# smaller than FIXABLE_ONLY_MAX_FILES: KNOWLEDGE.md's own measurement ("fixable-only mode's real
# per-file cost is ~20k tokens, not the 3-5k config.env estimated", 2026-09-07) means a run-start
# floor already at ~200k of a 300k ceiling (probes + 2b-bis + the knowledge profile + the checklist,
# per the 2026-09-09/17 addenda) leaves little room; adding a 3-5 file slot on top of that would
# crowd out the ordinary per-file deep scan almost entirely. 2 files (~40k) is a budget GUESS, not a
# measurement - `Q57 slot cost: <tokens> for <N> files` in the ledger note is what lets this be
# re-tuned from real data, same discipline as FIXABLE_ONLY_MAX_FILES.
# 0 disables the additive slot and restores the pre-2026-09-18 "Q57 only via rotation luck" behaviour
# for a NOT-throttled run; the throttled case is unaffected by this variable.
COVERAGE_SLOT_MAX_FILES=2

# LOW-COVERAGE CAMPAIGN (SKILL.md Step 3-quinquies; added 2026-09-27, operator: "can you please in
# the future handle also the missing lines?"). The Q57 slot above skips every file already reviewed
# this cycle, so a file reviewed early (Service.cs, 55.31% on 2026-09-27) was invisible to coverage
# work until the next cycle reset. The campaign ignores reviewedFiles: every run, throttled or not,
# scripts/coverage-campaign.sh picks the ONE src/** file furthest below COVERAGE_CAMPAIGN_FLOOR_PCT
# (with at least COVERAGE_CAMPAIGN_MIN_MISSES missed lines, and no OPEN `tests` issue already naming
# it), groups its Codecov misses by method, and the run files up to COVERAGE_CAMPAIGN_MAX_ISSUES
# `[Tests]` issues (one per method group, `fixable`) for servy-fix to land on main and net48. While a
# file's issues are open it waits; the next pick sees the new numbers. The issues never touch the
# maintainer's plate, so the throttle does not gate this step.
#   FLOOR_PCT  - the operator's target; 80 is the figure he asked Service.cs to reach.
#   MIN_MISSES - below this, a file's gap is too small to be worth a campaign (Q57 covers it).
#   MAX_ISSUES - per run. One group costs ~5-10k tokens (read the ranges, seam check, dup-check,
#                write the body). 3 keeps it well inside the scan's ceiling; #7033-#7041 (9 groups,
#                one file) were written in one live session as the worked example.
# COVERAGE_CAMPAIGN_MAX_ISSUES=0 disables the step.
COVERAGE_CAMPAIGN_FLOOR_PCT=80
COVERAGE_CAMPAIGN_MIN_MISSES=30
COVERAGE_CAMPAIGN_MAX_ISSUES=3

# MAINTAINER-FIRST (operator, 2026-09-06 evening: "eerst alles doen van reacties, dan kijken dat de
# maintainer niet droogloopt, dan zien dat wij niet drooglopen"). The order of concerns in EVERY
# scan run, throttled or not: (1) reactions - Step 2a other people's unanswered issues AND Step 2b
# the maintainer's own unanswered comments on the routine's issues; (2) his queue - Step 2b-bis reads
# a slice of the maintainer-bound issues the routine itself posted and takes back what is actually
# the routine's (pure comment/XML-doc subject, own duplicate, already fixed upstream); (3) only
# then the routine's own supply - Step 3-quater's Q57 slot runs EVERY run now (COVERAGE_SLOT_MAX_FILES,
# additive) or, when throttled, IS the whole review (FIXABLE_ONLY_MAX_FILES); the full per-file review
# (Steps 3-bis/4-7/2c) follows it when not throttled.
#
# Cap on maintainer comments Step 2b reads and answers per run (each is a thread read plus a file
# region; closing notes like "All fixed." are filtered out by maintainer-replies.sh and never count).
MAINTAINER_REPLIES_MAX=5
# Cap on maintainer-bound issue bodies Step 2b-bis reads per run, walking the queue with a persistent
# cursor (ledger key maintainerTriageCursor). ~0.6-1k tokens per body; 15 walks ~270 issues in ~18
# hourly runs and then wraps. Raise it when the queue is far over the throttle, lower it when the
# full review needs the budget.
MAINTAINER_TRIAGE_MAX=15

# Disclosure policy for CRITICAL hardening findings (added 2026-09-01 with the Q42-Q56 bank).
#
# This tracker is PUBLIC and belongs to someone else. A finding that says "a standard local user
# reaches the service account this way" is, once posted, a published escalation path for a release
# that is still shipping - the maintainer gets no window to fix it first. Everything the routine
# posted before this bank existed was a correctness or quality finding, where that concern does not
# arise; it does arise now, so it gets an explicit knob instead of an implicit choice.
#
#   hold (default) - a Q42-Q56 finding rated Critical (principal U at LOW effort, ending in code
#                    execution as the service account, a credential disclosure, or arbitrary
#                    deletion) is NOT auto-posted. The run puts it in its Final summary and in the
#                    ledger's lastRunNote under the LESSONS: marker, so the operator can raise it
#                    with the maintainer directly. Everything below Critical posts normally.
#   post           - post it like any other finding.
#
# Independent of this setting, an issue body always describes the MECHANISM and the FIX and never a
# working exploit recipe. That is not only disclosure hygiene: it is the more useful text for the
# person who has to repair it.
HARDENING_CRITICAL_POST=hold

# GitHub login the routine assigns an issue to when the issue is the MAINTAINER's to
# resolve. Requested by the maintainer on 2026-08-02 ("Assign all the issues to me").
# Leave empty to disable assignment entirely.
ISSUE_ASSIGNEE=aelassas

# GitHub login the routine assigns an issue to when the issue is one the routine itself
# MAY and CAN fix (added 2026-09-05, operator instruction: "niets aan de maintainer
# assignen als jij het mag en kan oplossen"). The two fix-pass subjects are the only
# things that qualify - an open issue carrying the `tests` label (FIX_TESTS_ISSUES=1)
# and a comment/XML-doc defect as a subject (FIX_COMMENT_ISSUES=1) - AND only when a
# proof route exists: not under .github/** (the PAT has no workflow scope, nothing can
# land there), not Servy.test.ps1 (cannot reach 100% green on any runner the routine
# has), not in the ledger's blockedIssues, not needing src/**. Everything else stays
# ISSUE_ASSIGNEE's. Exactly ONE of the two is on an issue, never both (operator, later the
# same day: "verwijder de maintainer als ik ben assigned; als ik het niet kan oplossen,
# unassign me dan en assign het aan hem"): gh_assign swaps between them and a gate refusal
# in the fix pass swaps the issue back to ISSUE_ASSIGNEE. A third login is never touched.
# The full rule is in SKILL.md ("ASSIGNEE RULE" in Rules of Thumb).
#
# This is the routine's own account (the login the PAT belongs to). Leave empty to fall
# back to ISSUE_ASSIGNEE for everything, i.e. the pre-2026-09-05 behaviour.
#
# Initial state: on 2026-09-05 the operator had the 311 then-fixable open issues (302
# `tests`-labelled + 9 pure comment/XML-doc `enhancement` issues) assigned to this
# login alone in an interactive session; aelassas was removed from those 311 and stays
# the sole assignee of the other 297 (the 5 blocked/unlandable `tests` issues included).
FIX_ASSIGNEE=Christophe-Rogiers

# Context ceiling - the QUALITY guard, and the one that matters most.
#
# This is not about cost (there is no output-token budget any more). It measures what a run
# is HOLDING - the size of the conversation it must reason over. Attention over a very
# large context is measurably worse, and a long run gets sloppy in ways that are hard to
# notice from inside: half-remembering a file read 200 turns ago, trusting a check made
# much earlier, losing track of what was already verified.
#
# ⚠️ READ THE FLOOR BEFORE CHOOSING A NUMBER. This is an ABSOLUTE ceiling, so it must
# clear the run's fixed start-up load, which is large and unavoidable:
#     fresh session                     ~38000 tokens
#   + SKILL.md (~125 KB)                ~46000
#   + KNOWLEDGE.md - BY PROFILE since 2026-09-05 (scripts/knowledge-extract.sh):
#       fix-only run ~43 KB             ~11000   (was the whole 100 KB, ~25000)
#       scan run     ~39 KB             ~10000   (+ reference/checklist.md ~11000 at Step 3-bis)
#       nothing-to-do run                    0   (Step 1d-bis exits after the census)
#   + config.env                         ~7500
#   + the run-start API passes          ~20000
#   = FLOOR                            ~125000 (fix) / ~135000 (scan, checklist included)
#     tokens before the first file is opened; ~15000 for a run that finds no work.
#   The remaining big line is SKILL.md itself; it shrinks only when the routine is split
#   into a fix routine and a scan routine with their own skill files.
# Measured on the 2026-09-02 23h run: 38268 at the first turn, 159020 after nothing but
# the mandatory reading.
#
# Set below ~175000 and NO RUN CAN EVER SCAN A FILE. That is exactly what happened when
# this was briefly 150000: every hourly run started, ran its cheap mandatory passes,
# tripped the gate and stopped having reviewed nothing. Step 1a-quinquies now refuses to
# start on such a value instead of failing silently, but do not rely on that - do the
# arithmetic: USABLE WORKING ROOM = this number minus ~160000.
#
# 300000 leaves ~140000 of room. The "10-14 files at ~10-15k per file" this comment used to
# predict was WRONG - measured on 2026-09-03 across five real files, context grew 154628 ->
# 276470, i.e. ~24000 per file for files of 10-40 KB that need source cross-checks (opening
# the twin, resolving a symbol, running a dup-check). So 300000 buys about FIVE files per run,
# not ten to fourteen. Budget accordingly: a 17-file worklist takes three or four runs.
# Raise it for longer runs, lower it if late-run findings look sloppy - but never below
# ~175000, and expect ~0 files at that end.
#
# THE REAL FIX is to stop re-reading 63000 tokens of instructions every hour: split
# SKILL.md so the reference material (the 57 questions, D1-D41, the wiki sweep commands)
# is consulted on demand rather than loaded in full. That would cut the floor roughly in
# half and make this ceiling mean what it says. Not done yet - it is a real refactor.
#
# Set to 0 to disable the check entirely.
MAX_CONTEXT_TOKENS=300000

# Reasoning effort - NOT read by the skill; documented here because it is the second token
# lever and it lives outside this file (added 2026-09-05).
#
# Claude Code sets effort PER SESSION, never per step: `/effort <level>`, the `--effort` flag,
# the CLAUDE_CODE_EFFORT_LEVEL environment variable, or `effortLevel` / `modelSettings` in
# settings.json (docs: code.claude.com/docs/en/model-config). The routine settings expose a
# model picker but no effort picker, so for a cloud routine the way in is the environment's
# variables (Code -> Environments -> servy-gh -> Environment variables), the same place
# SERVY_SIGNING_KEY lives. A run cannot change it from the inside; SKILL.md Step 2b's LEAN
# MODE is the in-run half of this lever (fewer, consolidated tool calls, no narration).
#
# Intended values, to apply when the routine is split (see KNOWLEDGE.md):
#   servy-fix   CLAUDE_CODE_EFFORT_LEVEL=medium   mechanical work under mechanical proofs
#   servy-scan  CLAUDE_CODE_EFFORT_LEVEL=high     the deep scan is where the judgement is
# While ONE routine still does both, leave it at the default (high): lowering it would lower
# the scan too, and the scan is the part that must not get sloppy. Levels on Opus 5 / Fable
# 5.1 / Sonnet 5: low, medium, high, xhigh, max; default high.

# Quiet hours: REMOVED on 2026-08-20 at the operator's request. The gate had
# already been disabled on 2026-08-13 (START == END) and the code is now gone
# from SKILL.md, so QUIET_HOURS_TZ / QUIET_HOURS_START / QUIET_HOURS_END are no
# longer read by anything. Do not re-add them here expecting an effect: nothing
# sources them any more. The cron expression in the Claude Code scheduled-task
# settings is now the only thing that decides when the routine runs — if a
# window should stay free, change the cron.

# Scan priority queue (added 2026-08-28). scripts/upstream-priority.sh promotes files the
# maintainer added or edited upstream to the head of the scan queue, ahead of the rotated
# remainder, so a newly added or freshly fixed file is reviewed on the fresh context instead of
# waiting for its rotation offset to come round.
#
# PRIORITY_PROMOTE_CAP - the most files one run may promote. This exists so a large refactor
# cannot turn the entire worklist into priority entries, which would suppress the rotation the
# coverage model depends on. Added files are promoted ahead of modified ones: a never-reviewed
# file outranks a re-review.
PRIORITY_PROMOTE_CAP=25

# PRIORITY_MAX_PROMOTE_KB - do not promote a file larger than this.
#
# THIS GOVERNS PROMOTION ONLY, NOT ELIGIBILITY. A file over the threshold is still reviewed; it
# just does not get to jump the queue, and is reached by normal rotation like everything else.
#
# Why it exists: CHANGELOG.md is 471 KB against a 200 KB per-run byte cap, and it changes on
# EVERY release. Without a threshold it would be promoted every release, deferred every time as
# too big to share a run, and would eventually consume an entire run by itself to review a
# changelog. The default of 100 KB is half the per-run byte cap, i.e. "a promoted file must be
# able to share its run with others". Raising it lets bigger files queue-jump at the cost of
# crowding out the rest of that run; lowering it restricts promotion to small files.
PRIORITY_MAX_PROMOTE_KB=100

# Abandoned-lock recovery window, in minutes, for the concurrency lock (SKILL.md
# Step 0). Two runs must never be active at once: on 2026-09-01 two hourly runs
# overlapped, built the identical worklist, and the second filed #6497 seventeen
# minutes after #6484 covered the same four predicates - the GitHub search index
# behind gh_dupcheck lags a few minutes, so the dup-check could not see it.
#
# THIS IS NOT AN OVERLAP DETECTOR. A healthy run releases its lock at every exit
# door; this value only decides how long a lock left behind by a run that DIED -
# killed, crashed, network cut - keeps blocking its successors.
#
# Do not shorten it to "catch overlaps faster". A run that is genuinely still
# working can legitimately take a while, and a short window steals a healthy
# run's lock out from under it, which recreates the exact double-post this
# mechanism exists to prevent. Raise it if real runs start running longer.
#
# The cost, stated plainly: at hourly firing, one run that dies without releasing
# blocks the next (180 -> two of them, 120 -> one). A blocked run costs an hour of
# coverage; an overlapping pair costs the maintainer a duplicate issue to triage.
# The trade is deliberate.
#
# 180 -> 120 on 2026-09-04 at the operator's request ("kan je de lock opheffen na
# 2u?"), with the anti-shortening warning above weighed against measured evidence
# rather than waved away:
#   - A dead run really does cost multiple runs. The 12:05:21Z run died without
#     releasing and its lock was reclaimed at exactly 15:05:21Z, blocking THREE
#     hourly runs (13:05, 13:24, 14:05) that each exited LOCKED having done nothing.
#   - Healthy runs are nowhere near the window. Measured the same day from the
#     ledger's own acquire/release commits: 15:05:36 -> 15:26:33 (~21 min) and
#     16:03:25 -> 16:22:39 (~19 min). 120 minutes is still ~6x the observed run
#     length, so the "stealing a healthy run's lock" hazard stays remote.
# RE-CHECK THIS BEFORE SHORTENING FURTHER, and raise it again if runs get longer:
# the fix passes are the variable part (a batched comments push plus Windows CI
# polling is the plausible way a run grows past two hours). The ledger doubles as
# the measurement - `git log --format='%ci %s' origin/ledger` shows every
# acquire and every release, so run duration is one command away.
LOCK_STALE_MINUTES=120

# Double-start debounce, in minutes (added 2026-09-03 at the operator's request:
# a safeguard against a human accidentally clicking "run now" twice).
#
# The concurrency lock above already stops two runs that OVERLAP: on a double
# click both runs try to push the lock, git accepts exactly one, and the loser
# exits with LOCKED. This value covers the other shape, where the first click
# produced a SHORT run (throttled, blocked, or itself LOCKED) that had already
# released the lock before the second click reached it. acquire_lock compares
# the ledger's lastRun (the previous run's final write) with the clock and
# exits with DEBOUNCED when the previous run finished less than this many
# minutes ago. A deliberate "run now" right after a completed run therefore
# has to wait this long; keep it short. 0 disables the debounce only - the
# lock stays.
MIN_RUN_INTERVAL_MINUTES=5

# Branch of THIS control repo that holds the cross-run coverage ledger
# (coverage.json). An orphan branch containing only that file; the routine's
# push_ledger helper commits to it and nothing else. Switched from OneDrive
# to GitHub on 2026-08-04 at the operator's request; the OneDrive path is
# fully retired (no fallback).
LEDGER_BRANCH=ledger

# Branch of THIS control repo that carries comment/XML-doc fixes from the scan routine to the
# fix routine (added 2026-09-05 with the routine split, operator decision: "in de scan fixen,
# landen in de fix-routine"). An orphan branch: one directory per entry with patch.diff,
# meta.json and a ready-to-post body.md. The scan routine writes (handoff_put), the fix routine
# lands and removes (handoff_take). Nothing else goes on it.
HANDOFF_BRANCH=handoff

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE ROUTINE SPLIT (2026-09-05, operator: "ok akkoord splits maar op"). Two hourly routines,
# each with its own skill, lock and ledger slots; see KNOWLEDGE.md "The routine split":
#
#   servy-fix    .claude/skills/servy-fix/SKILL.md    model Opus (operator choice), effort medium
#                housekeeping, label audit, backfill sweep, pending landings, handoff landing,
#                the fix pass. The ONLY writer on aelassas/servy.
#   servy-scan   .claude/skills/servy-scan/SKILL.md   model Fable 5 (operator choice), effort high
#                throttle, unanswered user issues (operator: "2a zou ik door scan laten doen"),
#                worklist, pre-pass, deep scan, cross-file, completeness, wiki, residual sweep,
#                post flush. Never commits to aelassas/servy; comment fixes go to the handoff
#                branch, tests findings are posted with `fixable`.
#
# The two routines fire on the hour and the operator cannot offset them by minutes, so they
# routinely start in the SAME minute. That is designed for: separate lock slots (locks.fix,
# locks.scan) that never exclude each other, a per-key ledger merge (own slot local-wins,
# other slots remote-wins), and a handoff branch of independent directories. Nothing in either
# skill waits for the other.
#
# The legacy debug-servy routine retires itself: once the ledger carries lastRuns.fix or
# lastRuns.scan it prints RETIRED at Step 0 and exits, and its nameless lock excludes the named
# ones (and vice versa) while both still exist. Turn the old routine off after the first
# successful run of each new one; delete .claude/skills/debug-servy afterwards. (Done 2026-09-27.)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# tests-issue FIX PASS (SKILL.md Step 2b). Added 2026-09-02 after the
# maintainer authorised it in writing:
#   "Let Claude fix all 'tests' issues on main branch ... once tests pass 100%
#    commit with your GitHub username ... Apply the same fixes to net48 branch
#    ... No PR ... This workflow should be triggered manually ... not
#    automatically on every new 'tests' issue. As for 'non-tests' issues, don't
#    touch them, I prefer to work on them manually."
#
# 0 = review and post only.
# 1 = the fix pass is armed, including for scheduled runs.
#
# SET TO 1 BY THE OPERATOR ON 2026-09-02, deliberately and with the tension known.
# The maintainer asked for a manual trigger ("not automatically on every new 'tests'
# issue"); the operator decided the routine should fix on every run instead, because
# the tracker gains roughly 10-13 issues an hour and a hand-triggered pass cannot keep
# up. Nothing about the SAFETY gates changes: a fix still only lands when the proving
# test runs 100% green on this machine, still touches test code only, still commits
# under a human identity, and still gets verified by the branch's own CI afterwards.
# What changed is only who pulls the trigger.
#
# ⚠️ This is a deviation from what the maintainer put in writing. If he objects, set it
# back to 0 - that alone restores the agreed behaviour, no other change needed.
#
# The pass only lands what it can RUN GREEN on the run's own machine. On Linux
# that is the PowerShell suites; C# needs Microsoft.WindowsDesktop.App, which has
# no Linux build, so `dotnet test` yields "Zero tests ran" and no C# test fix may
# be committed from there. Details in SKILL.md Step 2b and KNOWLEDGE.md.
FIX_TESTS_ISSUES=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# COMMENTS & XML-DOC FIX PASS (SKILL.md Step 2b). Added 2026-09-04 on the
# operator's instruction:
#   "alvorens je open test issues begint te fixen, kan je aub deze stap uitvoeren:
#    fix all comments and xmldoc issues on both main branch and net48 branch and
#    commit with your username? This will save me some time as there are many
#    comments issues."
#
# 0 = leave comment/xmldoc issues alone (the pre-2026-09-04 behaviour).
# 1 = the pass is armed and runs BEFORE the tests fix pass.
#
# SCOPE WIDENED 2026-09-04 EVENING - this knob now gates TWO things, not one:
#   (a) the open [Comments] backlog, inside the single FIX PASS (Step 2b), and
#   (b) NEW comment/xmldoc findings the review turns up, which are now FIXED where they
#       are found and never posted (SKILL.md FIX-DON'T-FILE + Step 4b).
# Operator instruction: "gelieve ook geen [comments] meer te posten op gh, fix ze meteen
# zoals je voor test label doet." Before this, the routine cleared the backlog in the same
# run in which the scan filed fresh [Comments] issues - the two halves cancelled out. Same
# correction the tests label got on 2026-09-03, and the same fallback: if a gate refuses
# (the diff would touch an executable line, the file will not parse, or ctx_gate fix says
# no room) the finding is posted exactly as before. Setting this to 0 restores BOTH
# behaviours at once - backlog untouched and new findings posted.
#
# ✅ THE MAINTAINER AGREED TO THIS ON 2026-09-04 - it is settled, not a standing question.
# It does widen the 2026-09-02 consent ("tests issues only", with "As for 'non-tests'
# issues, don't touch them, I prefer to work on them manually"): 98 of the 118 candidates
# the day it was added carry 'enhancement', so all of them were previously hands-off. The
# run that opened the pass flagged that tension explicitly instead of quietly proceeding,
# and the operator came back with his agreement. Runs should now just work the pass - no
# hedging on the tracker, no re-asking.
#
# What is settled is ONE SUBJECT - a defect in a comment or an XML doc block - not a label,
# and not the code those comments describe. Set this to 0 if he changes his mind; that alone
# restores the previous behaviour, no other change needed.
#
# The safety property is different from the tests pass and stronger: a comment fix is
# gated on a mechanically-proven COMMENT-ONLY DIFF (scripts/comment-only-diff.sh), so
# it cannot change behaviour and there is nothing for a test to prove. The pass still
# builds/parses every file it touches, because a malformed XML doc or a mangled <# #>
# block IS a real breakage.
FIX_COMMENT_ISSUES=1

# The two fix gates. A fix is not one indivisible expensive thing - it splits at
# the push, and only the half AFTER the push is unabandonable:
#
#   steps 1-6  verify, baseline, edit, mutation proof, local commit, cherry-pick
#              -> all in a clone on a VM that is wiped. Abandoning is FREE.
#   step 7+    push, poll CI on both branches, fix red, close the issue
#              -> a commit on the maintainer's main. NOT abandonable.
#
# So the binding gate is ctx_gate push, immediately before the push, and it only
# has to reserve the landing. ctx_gate fix is a cheap pre-flight that avoids
# starting obviously-doomed work; a false there costs nothing.
#
# This replaced a single 25000-token whole-fix reservation on 2026-09-03 evening.
# That version was too conservative in the direction that matters here: it refused
# to START fixes that would in fact have landed fine, which works against "fixen
# tot alles is opgelost". Splitting the gate means more fixes land per run at the
# same real safety, because the expensive-but-throwaway half is no longer reserved
# for as though it were permanent.
#
# FIX_HEADROOM_TOKENS - the throwaway half (pre-flight only, non-binding).
FIX_HEADROOM_TOKENS=12000

# FIX_LANDING_TOKENS - the half that must always fit: push, poll test.yml on each
# branch pushed, act on a red result, close the issue with a comment. This is the
# number to raise if a run ever ends between a push and a green CI read; it is the
# only one of the two whose exhaustion can leave state behind on someone else's
# repository. Both are estimates until real fixes are measured - the ~3000-6000
# figure recorded for past fixes is OUTPUT tokens, a different quantity from
# context held.
FIX_LANDING_TOKENS=8000

# Context tokens held back from the scan so the post queue can always be sent.
# Added 2026-09-03 evening, and it is load-bearing for the "post last" order.
#
# When each finding was posted the moment it was found, hitting the context ceiling
# cost only the files not yet scanned. Now that posting is deferred to the very end,
# hitting the ceiling mid-scan would ALSO throw away every finding already made -
# the queue lives in /tmp on a VM that is wiped after the run, so unsent is lost.
#
# ctx_gate scan stops the scan at (MAX_CONTEXT_TOKENS - POST_FLUSH_RESERVE -
# 1000 per queued finding), so the reservation grows as the queue does. A run that
# queues ten findings holds back ~25000; one that queues nothing holds back 15000
# and scans almost to the ceiling.
#
# Too low and findings die unsent. Too high and the scan stops early for nothing.
# 15000 covers the flush overhead itself; the per-finding term covers the bodies.
POST_FLUSH_RESERVE=15000

# Branches the fix pass commits to, in order. Note they are NOT structurally
# identical: net48 has no Directory.Build.props, global.json or build-config.ps1.
FIX_BRANCHES="main net48"

# Commit identity for the fix pass. "commit with your GitHub username (not Claude
# or something else - this is important - no Claude mentioned in code, comments or
# issues)". No Co-Authored-By, no session URL, no tool attribution anywhere.
#
# THE EMAIL MUST BE AN ADDRESS GITHUB CAN MAP TO THE ACCOUNT, or every commit the
# fix pass lands is orphaned (changed 2026-09-05). Until today this was
# christophe.rogiers@gmail.com, which is not on the Christophe-Rogiers account at
# all - the account's single verified address is a different one. GitHub therefore
# could not attribute the commits: the API reports
#
#     verified=false  reason=no_user  author=null
#
# and the web UI renders the author as plain text with "No user is associated with
# the committer email", on every commit the routine has ever pushed (100+ on each
# of main and net48).
#
# The noreply address below is the one GitHub itself designates for this account
# ("All web-based Git operations will be linked to ..."). It is always mapped, so
# reason=no_user cannot recur.
#
# Why not simply add the gmail address to the account instead: "Keep my email
# addresses private" is On, and the companion setting "Block command line pushes
# that expose my email" blocks a push whose author email is a PRIVATE address of
# the account. Adding the gmail address would arm exactly that block against this
# routine's own pushes. It works today only because GitHub does not know the
# address is his. The noreply form sidesteps the whole interaction.
#
# THE EXISTING COMMITS WERE REPAIRED FROM THE ACCOUNT SIDE, not from here.
# Attribution is resolved at render time from the email -> account mapping, so the
# operator added christophe.rogiers@gmail.com to the account as a verified address
# on 2026-09-05, after this change, and every past commit linked at once
# (reason went no_user -> unknown_key, author went null -> Christophe-Rogiers).
# Re-authoring the pushed history was never an option: rewriting main and net48 on
# aelassas/servy is forbidden and would break every existing checkout.
#
# THE "Unverified" BADGE - two diagnoses of it were wrong in one day, so read this
# rather than re-deriving it.
# The commits ARE signed (diagnosis 1, "unsigned", was wrong). The Claude Code
# environment signs every commit itself: ~/.gitconfig carries commit.gpgsign=true,
# gpg.format=ssh and gpg.ssh.program pointing at the environment manager binary;
# the local .pub file is empty and the private half never exists on the VM.
# Measured 2026-09-05 over 120 commits on main: ONE stable ssh-ed25519 key,
# SHA256:32dP45eSMmVSt/G/CGvcxl/P+MO3Nwj9xeTh/GSA2wc, across all 99 routine
# commits from 2026-09-03 onward (the 21 unsigned ones are the maintainer's own).
# GitHub reports reason=unknown_key because that key is not on the author's account.
#
# BUT IT CANNOT BE PUT THERE (diagnosis 2, "register it and everything turns
# Verified retroactively", was also wrong). GitHub requires an SSH key to be unique
# across all of GitHub, and adding this one fails with "Key is already in use":
# it is registered somewhere the operator does not control - verified absent from
# his own account (both key types), from his other account, and from the repo's
# deploy keys. All evidence says Anthropic holds that registration. Consequence:
# the 99 existing commits stay Unverified unless Anthropic releases the key; no
# setting on the operator's side changes that, vigilant mode included (a signature
# with an unknown key shows Unverified regardless).
#
# THE WAY OUT, FOR NEW COMMITS: sign with a key the operator CAN register.
# scripts/sign-setup.sh does it, driven by a secret that lives in the CLOUD
# ENVIRONMENT (Code -> Environments -> servy-gh -> Environment variables), NOT in
# this file - it is a private key and this file is public:
#
#   SERVY_SIGNING_KEY   the private half of an ed25519 pair, pasted verbatim (or
#                       base64 -w0 of it if the UI flattens newlines). Its public
#                       half goes on the author's GitHub account as a SIGNING key,
#                       a separate choice from an authentication key in the same
#                       form. Generate the pair on your own machine, never in a
#                       session: a key that passed through a transcript is public.
#
# Unset -> nothing changes, commits keep landing Unverified as before. Set -> the
# fix clone signs with it (repo-local config beats ~/.gitconfig) and
# sign-setup.sh --verify refuses a push whose tip does not carry it. The author
# email below must map to the account holding the signing key; the noreply
# address guarantees that.
FIX_COMMIT_NAME=Christophe-Rogiers
FIX_COMMIT_EMAIL=47819969+Christophe-Rogiers@users.noreply.github.com

# May a run dispatch .github/workflows/test.yml on aelassas/servy?
#
# GRANTED 2026-09-02: "Yes you can trigger test.yml via workflow_dispatch on a temp
# branch but make sure it does not trigger codecov and coveralls steps." The maintainer
# then added the opt-in himself on BOTH branches, and the path was verified end to end
# the same day (run 33683932240 on a temp branch off main):
#   - both jobs green, x64 and ARM64, full suite executed;
#   - all five upload steps SKIPPED - Merge and normalize, Import Codecov GPG,
#     Upload to Codecov, Upload to Coveralls, Notify Metrics.
# The gate is `github.event_name != 'workflow_dispatch' || inputs.upload_coverage`, with
# the input defaulting to false, so a dispatch that omits it never uploads. NEVER pass
# upload_coverage=true - that is his to tick, not ours.
#
# This is what makes a C# test fix verifiable at all: it cannot run on this Linux VM, so
# a dispatched run on a temp branch is the only way to see it green BEFORE it reaches
# main or net48. See SKILL.md Step 2b for the temp-branch procedure (test-project.yml, ~2 min per branch).
#
# A push to main or net48 triggering their own test.yml is NOT a dispatch and needs no
# permission; that path stays available and does upload coverage, as it should.
ALLOW_CI_DISPATCH=1

# How many days a blockedIssues entry survives before it is released for ONE
# re-evaluation (blocked_expire, Step 1a-ter, before the backfill sweep).
#
# A block is a deferral - "a gate refused this, do not re-derive it every hour" - but
# until 2026-09-12 nothing ever undid one: prune_blocked drops only CLOSED issues and
# capability_resync releases only `no-workflow-scope`. Since the sweep keeps every
# blocked issue assigned to the maintainer and fix_candidates enumerates only issues
# assigned to FIX_ASSIGNEE, a judgement class was permanent by construction. 14 open
# `tests` issues sat that way for six days while every run reported "nothing to do".
# Expiry gives the surrounding code, the tooling and the scope a chance to have changed.
# If the same gate refuses again the entry is simply re-recorded with a fresh date, so
# the cost is at most one re-evaluation per issue per BLOCK_REVIEW_DAYS.
BLOCK_REVIEW_DAYS=14

# How often the operator's consent-stall PushNotification may repeat itself while the stall
# is otherwise unchanged. See stall_ping_decide in scripts/helpers.sh and Step 4 of the
# servy-fix skill.
#
# consent_stall_check is a LEVEL detector: it exits 1 on every run for as long as the
# consent-bounded entries exist. Step 4 read that as "ping the operator, every run", and on
# 2026-09-12 that was measured: the 21:16Z run pinged {6139,6517,6646} and the 21:34Z run
# found byte-identical state and was about to send the same notification again - then every
# run after it, all night, until the maintainer answered. The 🛑 rule wants a stall to REACH
# a human; an hourly duplicate is how it stops reaching them.
#
# So the ping now fires on the EDGE: when the stall changes shape, when an issue in it has no
# open question on GitHub (no channel to the maintainer left), and unconditionally once every
# STALL_REPING_DAYS so a forgotten stall always resurfaces. Lower it if a stall has ever gone
# unnoticed; this is a notification-cadence knob, never a consent gate.
STALL_REPING_DAYS=3

# How many days before a consent stall with NO CHANNEL TO THE MAINTAINER is resurfaced
# (scripts/helpers.sh, stall_ping_decide). Shorter than STALL_REPING_DAYS on purpose.
#
# Added 2026-09-24. The "no open question on GitHub" arm of stall_ping_decide used to return a
# PING before the ping memory was read at all, so it had no edge state whatsoever. Measured on
# {6271,6273,6279}: the question covering all three was asked on #6278 and ANSWERED, and
# openQuestions is keyed by the issue a question was asked ON rather than by every issue it
# COVERS - so those three numbers can never appear in it, the arm matched on every run, and the
# operator would have received a byte-identical ping every hour for as long as the block held.
# That is the same hourly duplicate STALL_REPING_DAYS exists to prevent, arriving through the
# one branch that skipped it.
#
# The arm's intent is still honoured: a stall the maintainer cannot even be asked about IS more
# urgent, so it resurfaces on this shorter horizon instead of bypassing the horizon. Raise it to
# match STALL_REPING_DAYS if daily is too loud; a notification-cadence knob, never a consent gate.
STALL_NOCHANNEL_REPING_DAYS=1

# How many days before the IDLE ping is resurfaced (scripts/helpers.sh, idle_decide).
#
# The exact counterpart of STALL_REPING_DAYS above, and added 2026-09-20 because idle_decide had
# the duplicate-suppression half of that design and not the resurfacing half. Once IDLE-NOTIFIED
# was written into a note head, every later idle run read it and returned SILENT - forever, for as
# long as the condition held. Measured that day: the routine was structurally idle (0 open
# `tests` issues, 128 open `enhancement` issues it may not touch, and a verified OWNER grant on
# #6646 it cannot record from inside a cloud run), the operator was pinged once on the edge into
# that state, and every run after it was correctly, permanently silent while nothing moved.
#
# Suppressing a duplicate is right; going quiet on a condition nobody has acted on is the 🛑
# rule's failure mode. Same knob, same default, same meaning as STALL_REPING_DAYS: a
# notification-cadence knob, never a consent gate.
IDLE_REPING_DAYS=3

# How many days before a STUCK MAINTAINER GRANT is resurfaced to the operator
# (scripts/helpers.sh, question_ping_decide). Added 2026-09-26.
#
# The fourth counterpart of STALL_REPING_DAYS, and added for the same reason IDLE_REPING_DAYS was:
# the condition had a report and no notifier. `question_stuck` exits 1 while any openQuestions
# entry is `answered` and never closed - a grant the maintainer gave that the routine could not
# act on by itself, so only a HUMAN action clears it - but it writes solely to the Final summary
# and the ledger note, which no human reads. Measured on #6278: answered by the OWNER on
# 2026-09-23, named by `question_stuck` on every run since, and it reached the operator exactly
# once, because `idle_decide` happened to fire the same run and the ping was written to carry it.
# KNOWLEDGE.md called that "only by luck" and prescribed a `questionPing` detector; three days
# passed without one, including a run that materially REFRAMED the entry and wrote the reframing
# into the ledger note alone.
#
# A grant only a human can land is the thing that must keep resurfacing, so this is deliberately
# NOT the once-only milestone_ping shape. Same knob, same default, same meaning as
# STALL_REPING_DAYS and IDLE_REPING_DAYS: a notification-cadence knob, never a consent gate.
QUESTION_REPING_DAYS=3

# May the fix pass change src/** when a `tests`-labelled issue requires it?
#
# GRANTED 2026-09-12. The maintainer's original consent (2026-09-02, "Let Claude fix all
# 'tests' issues ... As for 'non-tests' issues, don't touch them") scopes by ISSUE. The
# routine had re-scoped it by FILE ("test code only"), which is not what he wrote, and
# that narrowing froze 15 tests issues for six days while he waited for them. Restated
# through the operator on 2026-09-12: "I want all 'tests' issues to be fixed before
# moving to other issues."
#
# This does NOT open src/** generally. It applies only to an issue carrying the `tests`
# label, only to the smallest seam that lets the test reach the branch, only in the same
# commit as the test, and only under the same dispatch + mutation proof on both branches.
# A production change bigger than a seam (a design decision, an API change, a behavioural
# fix) is still the maintainer's: block it and say what you would have written.
FIX_TESTS_ALLOW_SRC=1

# May the fix pass write TESTS for an issue that does NOT carry the `tests` label?
#
# GRANTED 2026-09-20 by the maintainer, in answer to the routine's own question on #6646
# (asked 2026-09-12, answered 2026-09-20T10:37:27Z, author_association OWNER):
#
#   "Go ahead. You have permission to write tests for issues labelled "enhancement" or
#    whatever label is."
#   https://github.com/aelassas/servy/issues/6646#issuecomment-5749274591
#
# The question he answered was explicit about both halves of what it asked: "does that
# permission follow the kind of work or the label? ... when an issue asks for a test but is
# not labelled `tests`, may this routine write and land that test under the same proof
# obligations it uses for a `tests` issue (test-project.yml green on a temp branch, plus a
# mutation run proving the new assertion can fail, on both `main` and `net48`)?" So the
# consent of 2026-09-02 follows the KIND OF WORK, not the label, and it arrives carrying
# those proof obligations - they are not relaxed by this gate, they are the reason it was
# granted.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO. It widens the TEST half only. The `src/**` seam allowance
# (FIX_TESTS_ALLOW_SRC) stays scoped to an issue carrying the `tests` label, because that is
# the issue class his 2026-09-12 sentence was about and this sentence says "write tests".
# For an issue of any other label the production code half remains his, exactly as
# 2026-09-02 put it ("As for 'non-tests' issues, don't touch them"): if the test cannot be
# written without a production change, that is an ask_maintainer, not a seam this gate
# covers. And the test must be one the ISSUE ASKS FOR - this is not a licence to add tests
# wherever the routine thinks coverage is thin.
FIX_TESTS_ANY_LABEL=1

# May the fix pass land a `[Central config]` issue, INCLUDING its production code?
#
# GRANTED 2026-09-22, relayed by the operator as the maintainer's own instruction:
#
#   "pas jezelf aan, in akkoord met maintainer : fix all \"[Central config]\" issues on the
#    main and net48 branches even if it touches production code. Some issues like the ones
#    related to Directory.Build.props are on main branch only and not in net48 branch."
#
# This is the FIRST consent that puts production code in scope for its own sake rather than
# as a seam under a test. The 2026-09-02 sentence ("As for 'non-tests' issues, don't touch
# them") is narrowed by it for exactly one issue class and no other: an issue whose TITLE
# carries the `[Central config]` tag, case-insensitively (#5541 spells it "[Central Config]").
# Every such issue is labelled `enhancement` today, so the label is not the selector and must
# not become one - "all enhancement issues" is not what was granted.
#
# WHAT A `[Central config]` FIX IS. These issues all have one shape: a value that already has
# a named home is ALSO written as a literal somewhere else, so the two can drift. The fix is
# to make the second site read the first - a constant referenced instead of repeated, a
# property inherited instead of restated, a list built from one source instead of two. That
# is a production edit and it is now this routine's to make. What it is NOT: a redesign of
# where the single source of truth should live, a new configuration mechanism, or a rename of
# the existing constant. If the issue offers a CHOICE of where the value should centralise,
# that is the same fork the tests-seam rule already names - ask_maintainer, do not pick.
#
# PROOF. Unchanged and not relaxed: the affected test project green by test-project.yml on a
# temp branch, plus a mutation run, on EACH branch that gets the change. A `[Central config]`
# edit is behaviour-preserving by construction, so the mutation to reach for is the drift the
# issue describes - change the centralised value and show a test notices - rather than
# breaking the SUT. When no test covers the site at all, write one: FIX_TESTS_ANY_LABEL=1
# already permits the test half of an `enhancement` issue, and a centralisation with nothing
# asserting the two sites agree is the drift re-created one commit later.
#
# NET48. His sentence names the exception itself: "Some issues like the ones related to
# Directory.Build.props are on main branch only and not in net48 branch." `net48` has no
# Directory.Build.props, global.json, Directory.Packages.props or build-config*.ps1 (the list
# is in KNOWLEDGE.md, "net48 porting gotchas"), so `git cat-file -e origin/net48:<path>`
# before every port stays the rule and a miss is main-only, said plainly in the closing
# comment. That is NOT licence to skip net48 when the file DOES exist there.
#
# The safety rails are untouched: no PR, fork, tag, release, force-push, history rewrite,
# skipped test, empty commit, AI-attribution line, and no closing an issue this routine did
# not fix. 0 disables the gate and the routine goes back to leaving these on the maintainer.
FIX_CENTRAL_CONFIG_ISSUES=1

# May the fix pass land a `[Duplication]` issue, INCLUDING its production code?
#
# GRANTED 2026-09-23, relayed by the operator as the maintainer's own instruction:
#
#   "ik heb overlegd met de maitianer en dit moet gebeuren : fix all \"[Duplication]\" issues
#    even if it touches production code on main and net48 branches and only commit when
#    test-project.yml for the related project is green on main and net48 branches."
#
# Same shape as the `[Central config]` grant of 2026-09-22, and it is read the same way.
# The SELECTOR IS THE TITLE TAG `[Duplication]`, case-insensitively - not a label. All 31
# open ones carry `enhancement` today, and "all enhancement issues" is emphatically not what
# was granted; a pass that widens from the tag to the label has re-run the #5802 failure at
# the scale of a whole label.
#
# WHAT A `[Duplication]` FIX IS. These issues all have one shape: one piece of logic, data or
# markup exists in two or more copies that must be kept in step by hand, and the issue names
# the copies and the single home they should collapse into (a helper that already exists, a
# base class the neighbours already derive from, a constant already declared elsewhere). The
# fix is to make the second copy defer to the first with behaviour unchanged. What it is NOT:
# a redesign of where the shared thing should live, a new abstraction the issue does not
# name, or a behavioural change smuggled in beside the dedup. If the issue offers a CHOICE of
# which copy survives or where the shared code should go, that is the same fork the
# tests-seam rule already names - ask_maintainer, do not pick.
#
# PROOF. His sentence states its own gate and it is STRICTER than the default, so it is the
# binding one: "only commit when test-project.yml for the related project is green on main
# and net48 branches". No landing without a green test-project.yml dispatch for the affected
# test project on a temp branch cut from EACH branch that gets the change. The mutation run
# stays required on top of it, and because a dedup is behaviour-preserving by construction
# the mutation to reach for is the DRIFT the issue describes - make the surviving copy differ
# from what the deleted one did and show a test notices - not a break of the SUT. When no
# test covers the deduplicated site at all, write one: FIX_TESTS_ANY_LABEL=1 already permits
# the test half of an `enhancement` issue, and a dedup with nothing asserting the two sites
# agreed is the drift re-created one commit later.
#
# NET48. `git cat-file -e origin/net48:<path>` before every port, as always. Several of these
# issues name `.yml` workflow files or `setup/**` PowerShell that net48 does not carry (the
# list is in KNOWLEDGE.md, "net48 porting gotchas"); such an issue is main-only and the
# closing comment says so. That is NOT licence to skip net48 when the file DOES exist there,
# and his sentence names both branches explicitly.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT MOVE. `setup/**` production tooling stays out of scope under the tests
# rules, but a `[Duplication]` issue whose subject IS that tooling (e.g. publish-common.ps1 /
# build-common.ps1) is in scope under this gate - the selector is the title tag, and his
# sentence says "even if it touches production code". What has no proof route here still has
# none: a PowerShell suite is proven as the tests rules already prescribe.
#
# The safety rails are untouched: no PR, fork, tag, release, force-push, history rewrite,
# skipped test, empty commit, AI-attribution line, and no closing an issue this routine did
# not fix. 0 disables the gate and the routine goes back to leaving these on the maintainer.
FIX_DUPLICATION_ISSUES=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `[Control flow]` ISSUES - PRODUCTION CODE INCLUDED (write-access clause (f))
#
# GRANTED 2026-09-24, relayed by the operator as the maintainer's own instruction:
#
#   "fix all \"[Control flow]\" issues on main and net48 branches and only commit once
#    test-project.yml of the related project is green on both branches. Also update related
#    tests if necessary. this is alligned with the maintainerer."
#
# Same shape as the `[Central config]` grant of 2026-09-22 and the `[Duplication]` grant of
# 2026-09-23, and it is read the same way. The SELECTOR IS THE TITLE TAG `[Control flow]`,
# case-insensitively - not a label. All 5 open ones carry `enhancement` today, and "all
# enhancement issues" is emphatically not what was granted; a pass that widens from the tag to
# the label has re-run the #5802 failure at the scale of a whole label.
#
# WHAT A `[Control flow]` FIX IS. These issues have one shape: a branch, guard or catch is
# PROVABLY unreachable or PROVABLY constant on every path that can reach it, and the issue names
# the paths and shows why. The fix is to delete the dead half and nothing else. What it is NOT: a
# restructure of the surrounding method, a behavioural change smuggled in beside the deletion, or
# a guard removed because it "looks" redundant. The issue's own reachability argument is the
# warrant, and it is RE-VERIFIED AGAINST THE CURRENT FILE before anything is deleted - these
# issues age badly, because a later commit can add the very caller that makes the guard live
# again. If the issue offers a CHOICE (delete the guard, or keep it and make the caller honest),
# that is the same fork the tests-seam rule already names - ask_maintainer, do not pick.
#
# PROOF. His sentence states its own gate and it is STRICTER than the default, so it binds:
# "only commit once test-project.yml of the related project is green on both branches". No
# landing without a green test-project.yml dispatch for the affected test project on a temp
# branch cut from EACH branch that gets the change. The mutation run stays required on top of it.
# Because removing dead code is behaviour-preserving BY CONSTRUCTION, the mutation to reach for
# is the REACHABILITY CLAIM the issue makes, not a break of the SUT: make the surviving path do
# something different for the input the deleted branch used to handle, and show a test notices.
# "Also update related tests if necessary" is part of the grant and is usually where the work
# is: a test that existed only to drive the dead branch is updated or removed with it, and a site
# left with nothing asserting the surviving behaviour gets a test written under
# FIX_TESTS_ANY_LABEL=1, exactly as the dedup gate already requires.
#
# NET48. `git cat-file -e origin/net48:<path>` before every port, as always, and his sentence
# names both branches explicitly. A guard that is dead on `main` is NOT automatically dead on
# `net48`: that branch has its own callers and its own dialect, so the reachability argument is
# re-checked there against net48's own sources before the port is committed. When it does not
# hold, the port is skipped and the closing comment says exactly why - which is not licence to
# skip net48 when it does hold.
#
# The safety rails are untouched: no PR, fork, tag, release, force-push, history rewrite,
# skipped test, empty commit, AI-attribution line, and no closing an issue this routine did
# not fix. 0 disables the gate and the routine goes back to leaving these on the maintainer.
FIX_CONTROL_FLOW_ISSUES=1

# `[Enhancement]` ISSUES - PRODUCTION CODE INCLUDED (write-access clause (g))
#
# GRANTED 2026-09-25, by the OPERATOR, in a live session, verbatim:
#
#   "Can you please change yourself so that future runs dot this : fix all \"[Enhancement]\"
#    issues on main and net48 branches and only commit once test-project.yml of the related
#    project is green on both branches? Also update/add related tests if necessary with AAA
#    comments."
#
# PROVENANCE: ALIGNED WITH THE MAINTAINER, CONFIRMED IN A SECOND MESSAGE. The grant sentence above
# named no relay, so this gate was first recorded as operator-only. The operator confirmed the
# alignment immediately afterwards, same live session, 2026-09-25, verbatim:
#
#   "it is all alligned with the maintainer"
#
# So (g) stands on the same footing as (d), (e) and (f) - the operator relaying the maintainer -
# and a closing comment may say so. The one nuance worth keeping, because it is a fact about the
# record rather than a doubt about the grant: the alignment was stated as a SEPARATE confirmation
# rather than inside the instruction, so what is quotable as the maintainer's own wording is the
# confirmation sentence, not the four requirements. If he ever disputes a specific requirement,
# that is the seam to look at first.
#
# (g) remains the clause that most directly narrows his 2026-09-02 sentence ("As for 'non-tests'
# issues, don't touch them"), so a closing comment that has to explain why the routine touched an
# `enhancement`-labelled issue still says which consent it is acting under, rather than leaving
# him to reconcile the two himself.
#
# THE SELECTOR IS THE TITLE TAG `[Enhancement]`, CASE-INSENSITIVELY - NOT THE LABEL, and here
# that distinction carries more weight than in any earlier clause. Measured 2026-09-25: 20 open
# issues carry the TAG, 42 carry the `enhancement` LABEL. The 22-issue gap is the whole of the
# `[Robustness]`, `[Edge case]`, `[Root cause]`, `[Naming]`, `[Style]`, `[Whitespace]`,
# `[Complexity]`, `[Maintainability]`, `[Optimization]` and `[Quick win]` backlog, none of which
# is granted here. A pass that widens from the tag to the label takes 22 issues nobody granted, in
# one hour, and that is the #5802 failure at the largest scale this tracker allows.
#
# AND (g) IS NOT "EVERYTHING IS MINE NOW". After this clause the maintainer still owns: every
# issue whose title carries none of the four granted tags and which is not labelled `tests`, plus
# everything labelled `security`, `bug`, `documentation` or `feature-request` whatever its title.
# A run that reads (g) as the end of the consent boundary has misread it.
#
# WHAT AN `[Enhancement]` FIX IS. This tag is the scan routine's bucket for a code-quality defect
# that is not one of the more specific tags, so unlike (d), (e) and (f) it names no single
# transformation. What makes it safe is therefore NOT the shape of the work - it is the same three
# boundaries every earlier clause already carries, and they do all the load here:
#   1. The change is EXACTLY what the issue describes and nothing else. No neighbouring cleanup,
#      no refactor that reads nicer, no second issue folded in. One commit, one issue.
#   2. AN ISSUE OFFERING A CHOICE IS `ask_maintainer`, NEVER PICKED. This is the rule most likely
#      to fire under (g) and the one whose failure is most expensive. Surveyed 2026-09-25, several
#      of the 20 openly offer a fork (which of two homes a value gets, whether a seam should
#      expose a different type at all, whether a facade should be made immutable or documented as
#      advisory). A design decision has alternatives; a defect has one smallest form. If you
#      cannot tell which you are looking at, it is the fork.
#   3. A NEW CAPABILITY, AN API OR SCHEMA CHANGE, A RENAME OF A PUBLIC MEMBER, OR A BEHAVIOURAL
#      CHANGE THE ISSUE DOES NOT ITSELF SPELL OUT is not this clause's work, however small the
#      diff. Post it as a question and block it.
#
# PROOF. The operator's sentence states its own gate and it BINDS, exactly as (e) and (f) do:
# "only commit once test-project.yml of the related project is green on both branches". No landing
# without a green `test-project.yml` dispatch for the affected test project on a temp branch cut
# from EACH branch that gets the change.
#
# THE MUTATION PROOF IS THE ORDINARY ONE HERE, AND THIS IS THE ONE PLACE (g) DIFFERS MECHANICALLY
# FROM (d), (e) AND (f). Those three are behaviour-preserving BY CONSTRUCTION, which is why their
# mutation is aimed at the drift or the reachability claim rather than at the SUT. An
# `[Enhancement]` fix is frequently NOT behaviour-preserving - it adds a missing input guard,
# corrects which warning is emitted, or preserves state that was being discarded - so the mutation
# reverts to the `tests` shape: BREAK THE SUT AT THE SITE THE FIX CHANGED and show the covering
# test fails specifically. Do not reach for a drift mutation out of habit; on a behavioural fix it
# proves nothing.
#
# NO RELATED TEST PROJECT MEANS NO PROOF ROUTE, WHICH MEANS BLOCK - NOT A GUESS. The operator's
# gate names "the related project", and several of the 20 sit in WPF/XAML and design-time
# territory (`LogsView.xaml`, `DesignTimeMocks.cs`, the six `IValueConverter` implementations)
# that no test project reaches. When no `test-project.yml` dispatch can certify the change,
# `block_issue <n> no-proof-route` with a trace saying so. A green build is a compile check and
# was never a test result.
#
# "ALSO UPDATE/ADD RELATED TESTS IF NECESSARY WITH AAA COMMENTS" IS PART OF THE GRANT. A test that
# only asserted the old behaviour is updated with the fix; a site left with nothing asserting the
# new behaviour gets a test, in the SAME commit as the production change so one revert takes
# both. Every such test carries `// Arrange`, `// Act`, `// Assert` - the operator restated the
# maintainer's 2026-09-22 AAA instruction inside this grant, so it is doubly binding here, and it
# applies to a new case added inside an existing method as much as to a new method.
#
# NET48. `git cat-file -e origin/net48:<path>` before every port, as always, and the sentence
# names both branches. Re-read the cited construct against net48's OWN sources before porting:
# that branch has its own dialect and its own callers, and clause (f)'s lesson applies here with
# more force, not less, because an `[Enhancement]` fix often depends on what the surrounding code
# does rather than only on what one line says. When the construct is absent or already different
# there, the port is skipped and the closing comment says exactly why - which is NOT licence to
# skip net48 when it is present.
#
# The safety rails are untouched: no PR, fork, tag, release, force-push, history rewrite, skipped
# or disabled test, empty commit, AI-attribution line, and no closing an issue this routine did
# not fix. 0 disables the gate and the routine goes back to leaving these on the maintainer.
FIX_ENHANCEMENT_ISSUES=1
# (h) Service.cs COVERAGE SEAMS, PRODUCTION CODE INCLUDED (granted 2026-09-27, relayed by the
# operator in a live session as the maintainer's message, verbatim: "You have permission to edit
# Service.cs source code on both branches to increase code coverage as long as it does not break
# production code or change current logic. You can create seams, new interfaces, classes, and so
# on."). Full reading in servy-fix/SKILL.md clause (h). In one line: for a `tests` issue whose
# subject is src/Servy.Service/Service.cs, the routine may add seams (virtual wrappers, new internal
# interfaces/classes under src/Servy.Service/**, constructor overloads) WITHOUT asking, provided every
# production path calls exactly what it called before, no behavioural fix rides along, no public
# surface is added, no [ExcludeFromCodeCoverage] is used to move the number, and shared types outside
# Servy.Service are not edited. Proof: test-project.yml green for Servy.Service.UnitTests on BOTH
# branches + mutation + a before->after call list in the closing comment. 0 disables the gate and
# such seams go back to ask_maintainer.
FIX_SERVICE_CS_COVERAGE_SEAMS=1
# The scan side of the same grant: files whose "needs a production seam" coverage groups are filed as
# `tests` + fixable (Step 3-quinquies) instead of being skipped as unreachable. Space-separated
# repo-relative paths. It lists exactly the file the grant names; add another only with its own grant.
COVERAGE_SEAM_FILES="src/Servy.Service/Service.cs"
# PRIORITY FILES (maintainer, relayed by the operator in a live session 2026-09-28, verbatim: "Coverage is
# rising. We're on the good track. Could you prompt Claude to prioritize Servy.Service/Service.cs tests? I
# want to follow the Service.cs issues and review the changes after the commits."). ORDER, not scope: it
# grants nothing new. servy-scan Step 3-quinquies passes it to coverage-campaign.sh, which picks a listed
# file FIRST while it is below COVERAGE_CAMPAIGN_FLOOR_PCT, even while its own issues are open (each
# GROUP line then carries OPEN:#n tags so the scan does not duplicate one). servy-fix Step 3 lands the
# issues whose subject is a listed file BEFORE every other candidate. Space-separated repo paths; empty
# = no priority.
COVERAGE_PRIORITY_FILES="src/Servy.Service/Service.cs"


# KNOWLEDGE.md self-compaction (operator instruction, live session 2026-09-21: "detecteer de
# toekomstige scans zelf of je compactatie moet doen. dan doe je dat maar zonder mijn toestemming" -
# future scans decide FOR THEMSELVES whether a compaction pass is due, and land it without asking,
# same standing authorization push_knowledge already has). servy-scan Step 1a-sexies checks
# KNOWLEDGE.md's byte size on the fresh `origin/main` tip every run (one `git show`, no clone) and,
# when it is at or above this threshold, performs a compaction pass via `compact_knowledge` in
# scripts/helpers.sh. 0 disables the check entirely.
#
# 2026-09-21 first-ever compaction (this session, interactive, per the operator's own preference
# that the first pass run "ideally by an interactive session, not a scheduled run under its own
# token ceiling") took the file from ~1,027,000 bytes to roughly a third of that. 700000 sits
# comfortably above the immediate post-compaction size so ordinary hourly LESSONS: growth has real
# room before it retriggers, and well below the point where a `scan` profile read alone was
# measured eating the run's entire fixed floor (KNOWLEDGE.md addenda dated 2026-09-19/20/21, now
# folded into this compaction).
KNOWLEDGE_COMPACT_THRESHOLD_BYTES=700000

# Minimum days between two self-triggered compactions, so a single pass that does not fully clear
# the threshold (or ordinary growth from ongoing lessons) does not retrigger every single hour.
# Read from `compact(knowledge):`-prefixed commits on KNOWLEDGE.md's own git history - no ledger
# key needed, so this is not something ledger_merge has to own per-routine.
KNOWLEDGE_COMPACT_COOLDOWN_DAYS=7
